== Spiritual, Supernatural and Divine

1\. Affections that are truly spiritual and gracious arise from influences and operations on the heart that are #emph[spiritual, supernatural] and #emph[divine.]

I will explain what I mean by these terms, and from that their use for distinguishing affections that are spiritual from those that are not will appear.

We find that true saints, or those who are sanctified by the Spirit of God, are called #emph[spiritual] persons in the New Testament. And their being #emph[spiritual] is spoken of as their distinctive character, the thing that distinguishes them from those who are not sanctified. This is evident because those who are spiritual are set in opposition to natural men and carnal men. So the spiritual man and the natural man are set in opposition to each other in 1 Cor 2:14–15: “The #emph[natural] man does not accept the things that come from the Spirit of God. For they are foolishness to him, and he cannot know them, because they are spiritually discerned. The #emph[spiritual] man judges all things.” The Scripture explains itself to mean by a natural man an ungodly man, one who has no grace. So the apostle #emph[Jude,] speaking of certain #emph[ungodly men] who had crept in unnoticed among the saints (verse 4 of his epistle), says in verse 19, “These are #emph[sensual,] not having the Spirit.” The apostle gives this as the reason they behaved in the wicked way he had described. Here the word translated #emph[sensual] is ψυχικοὶ in the original, the very same word that is translated #emph[natural] in those verses of 1 #emph[Cor] chapter 2. In the same way, as the same discourse continues, two verses further on, #emph[spiritual men] are set against #emph[carnal men,] and the connection plainly shows that these mean the same as #emph[spiritual men] and natural men in the verses before: “Brothers, I could not address you#super[+] as spiritual, but as #emph[carnal]”; that is, as in great measure unsanctified. That by carnal the apostle means corrupt and unsanctified is abundantly evident from Romans 7:25 and 8:1, 4–9, 12–13; Gal 5:16 to the end; and Col 2:18. Now therefore, if by natural and carnal in these texts he meant unsanctified, then no doubt by spiritual, which is set against them, he means sanctified and gracious.

And as the saints are called spiritual in Scripture, so we also find that certain properties, qualities and principles are given the same epithet. So we read of a #emph[spiritual mind] (Rom 8:6–7), of #emph[spiritual wisdom] (Col 1:9), and of #emph[spiritual] blessings (Eph 1:3).

Now it may be observed that the epithet #emph[spiritual,] in these and other parallel texts of the New Testament, is not used to signify any relation of persons or things to the spirit or soul of man, as the spiritual part of man in contrast to the body, which is the material part. Qualities are not said to be spiritual because they have their seat in the soul and not in the body. For there are some properties that the Scripture calls #emph[carnal] or #emph[fleshly] which have their seat in the soul just as much as the properties that are called #emph[spiritual.] This is the case with pride and self-righteousness, and with a man’s trusting in his own wisdom, which the apostle calls #emph[fleshly] (Col 2:18). Nor are things called spiritual because they are occupied with things that are immaterial and not bodily. For the wisdom of the wise men and rulers of this world was occupied with spirits and immaterial beings; yet the apostle speaks of these men as #emph[natural men,] totally ignorant of the things that are #emph[spiritual] (1 Cor 2). But it is with relation to the #emph[Holy Spirit,] or #emph[Spirit of God,] that persons or things are called spiritual in the New Testament. #emph[Spirit,] as the word is used to signify the third person of the Trinity, is the noun from which the adjective #emph[spiritual] is formed in the holy Scriptures. So Christians are called spiritual persons because they are born of the Spirit, and because of the indwelling and holy influences of the Spirit of God in them. And things are called spiritual as they are related to the Spirit of God, as in 1 Cor 2:13–14: “And this is what we speak, not in words taught us by human wisdom, but in words taught by the Spirit, expressing spiritual things in spiritual words. The natural man does not accept the things that come from the Spirit of God.” Here the apostle himself expressly indicates that by #emph[spiritual things] he means #emph[the things that come from the Spirit of God,] and #emph[things taught by the Spirit.] The same is still more abundantly apparent from the whole context. Again, Rom 8:6: “To be #emph[carnally minded] is death, but to be #emph[spiritually minded] is life and peace.” In what follows, in the ninth verse, the apostle explains what he means by being carnally and spiritually minded, and shows that by being spiritually minded he means having the indwelling and holy influences of the Spirit of God in the heart: “You#super[+], however, are not #emph[in the flesh,] but #emph[in the Spirit,] if #emph[the Spirit of God dwells in you#super[+]]. And if anyone #emph[does not have the Spirit of Christ,] he does not belong to Christ.” The same is evident from the whole context. But time would fail me to bring forward all the evidence there is of this in the New Testament.

And it must be observed here that although persons and things are called spiritual with relation to the Spirit of God and his influences, yet not all persons who are subject to any kind of influence of the Spirit of God are ordinarily called spiritual in the New Testament. Those who have only the common influences of God’s Spirit are not called so in the places cited above, but only those who have the special, gracious and saving influences of God’s Spirit. This is evident because it has already been proved that by spiritual men is meant godly men, in contrast to natural, carnal and unsanctified men. And it is very plain that by spiritually minded (Rom 8:6) the apostle means graciously minded. The extraordinary gifts of the Spirit, which natural men might have, are sometimes called spiritual, because they are from the Spirit. Yet natural men, whatever gifts of the Spirit they had, were not called spiritual persons in the usual language of the New Testament. For it was not by having the gifts of the Spirit, but by having the virtues of the Spirit, that men were called spiritual. This is apparent from Galatians 6:1: “Brothers, if someone is caught in a trespass, you#super[+] who are spiritual should restore him with a spirit of meekness.” Meekness is one of the virtues the apostle had just spoken of in the verses immediately before, where he shows what the #emph[fruit of the Spirit] is. In the language of the New Testament, those qualities are said to be spiritual which are truly gracious and holy, and belong to the saints alone.

So when we read of spiritual wisdom and understanding (as in Col 1:9: “asking God to fill you#super[+] with the knowledge of His will #emph[in all spiritual wisdom and understanding]”), what is meant is the wisdom that is gracious and comes from the sanctifying influences of the Spirit of God. For no doubt by #emph[spiritual wisdom] is meant what is opposite to what the Scripture calls #emph[natural wisdom,] just as the #emph[spiritual man] is set against the #emph[natural man.] And therefore spiritual wisdom is no doubt the same as the wisdom from above that the apostle #emph[James] speaks of in Jas 3:17: “The wisdom from above is first of all pure, then peace-loving, gentle,” and so on. For the apostle sets this against natural wisdom in v. 15: “Such wisdom does not come from above, but is earthly, sensual.” The last word, in the original, is the same word that is translated #emph[natural] in 1 Corinthians 2:14.

So natural men may be the subjects of many influences of the Spirit of God, as is evident from many Scriptures, such as Num 24:2; 1 Sam 10:10, 11:6 and 16:14; 1 Cor 13:1–3; Heb 6:4–6; and many others. Yet in the sense of Scripture they are not spiritual persons. Nor are any of the effects, common gifts, qualities or affections that come from the influence of the Spirit of God on them called spiritual things. The great difference lies in these two things.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The Spirit of God is given to the true saints to dwell in them as his own lasting home, and to influence their hearts as a principle of new nature, or as a divine, supernatural spring of life and action. The Scriptures represent the Holy Spirit not only as moving the saints and influencing them from time to time, but as dwelling in them as his temple, his own home, and his everlasting dwelling place (1 Cor 3:16; 2 Cor 6:16; John 14:16–17). And he is represented as being so united there to the faculties of the soul that he becomes there a principle, or spring, of new nature and life.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So the saints are said to live by Christ living in them (Gal 2:20). Christ by his Spirit not only #emph[is] in them, but #emph[lives] in them, so that they live by his life. His Spirit is so united to them as a principle of life in them. They do not only drink living water; this #emph[living water becomes a well] or #emph[fount of water] in the soul, #emph[springing up to spiritual] and #emph[eternal life] (John 4:14), and so becomes a principle of life in them. The evangelist himself explains that this living water means the Spirit of God (John 7:38–39). The light of the sun of righteousness does not only shine on them, but is so imparted to them that they shine too, and become little images of the sun that shines on them. The sap of the true vine is not only conveyed into them as the sap of a tree may be conveyed into a vessel, but is conveyed as sap is from a tree into one of its living branches, where it becomes a principle of life. The Spirit of God being imparted and united to the saints in this way, they are properly named from it and are called #emph[spiritual.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[On the other hand, though the Spirit of God may influence natural men in many ways, yet because it is not imparted to them in this way, as an indwelling principle, they do not take any name or character from it; for since there is no union, it is not their own. Light may shine on a body that is very dark or black. Though that body is the subject of the light, yet because the light becomes no principle of light in it, so as to make the body shine, that body does not properly take its name from the light, so as to be called a #emph[luminous body.] In the same way the Spirit of God, acting on the soul only, without imparting itself to be an active principle in it, cannot give it the name #emph[spiritual.] A body that remains black may be said #emph[not to have light,] though the light shines on it. So natural men are said #emph[not to have the Spirit] (Jude 19): “#emph[sensual,]” or #emph[natural] (as the word is rendered elsewhere), “#emph[not having the Spirit.]”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Another reason why the saints and their virtues are called spiritual (and this is the principal thing) is that the Spirit of God, dwelling as a vital principle in their souls, produces there effects in which he exerts and imparts himself in his own #emph[proper nature.] Holiness is the nature of the Spirit of God; that is why he is called in Scripture the #emph[Holy Spirit.] Holiness, which is, as it were, the beauty and sweetness of the divine nature, is as much the proper nature of the Holy Spirit as heat is the nature of fire, or as sweetness was the nature of the holy anointing oil, which was the principal type of the Holy Spirit in the #emph[Mosaic] dispensation. Indeed, I may rather say that holiness is as much the proper nature of the Holy Spirit as sweetness was the nature of the sweet fragrance of that ointment. The Spirit of God so dwells in the hearts of the saints that there, as a seed or spring of life, he exerts and imparts himself in this sweet and divine nature of his. He makes the soul a partaker of God’s beauty and Christ’s joy, so that the saint truly has fellowship #emph[with] the Father and with his Son Jesus Christ, in having in this way the communion or participation #emph[of] the Holy Spirit. The grace that is in the hearts of the saints is of the same nature as the divine holiness, as far as it is possible for a holiness that is infinitely less in degree to be. It is like the brightness of a diamond that the sun shines on, which is of the same nature as the brightness of the sun, except that it is as nothing to it in degree. That is why Christ says in John 3:6, “That which is born of the Spirit is spirit”; that is, the grace that is begotten in the hearts of the saints is something of the same nature as that Spirit, and so is properly called a #emph[spiritual nature,] in the same way as what is born of the flesh is flesh, or what is born of corrupt nature is corrupt nature.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But the Spirit of God never influences the minds of natural men in this way. Though he may influence them in many ways, yet in none of his influences does he ever impart himself to them in his own proper nature. Indeed, he never acts in a way that disagrees with his nature, either on the minds of saints or of sinners. But the Spirit of God may act on men in agreement with his own nature without exerting his proper nature in the acts and exercises of their minds. The Spirit of God may act in such a way that his actions agree with his nature, and yet not impart himself at all in his proper nature in the effect of that action. For instance, the Spirit of God moved on the face of the waters, and there was nothing in that action that disagreed with his nature; yet he did not impart himself at all in that action. There was nothing of the proper nature of the Holy Spirit in that motion of the waters. And so he may act on the minds of men in many ways and not impart himself any more than when he acts on inanimate things.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So it is not only the manner of the #emph[relation] of the Spirit, who is the #emph[one who works,] to the subject of his working that is different: the Spirit works in the saints as dwelling in them, as an abiding principle of action, whereas he does not work on sinners in that way. The influence and #emph[working itself] is also different, and the #emph[effect produced] is exceedingly different. So not only are the persons called #emph[spiritual,] as having the Spirit of God dwelling in them; the qualities, affections and experiences that the Spirit produces in them are also #emph[spiritual.] In this they differ vastly, in their nature and kind, from everything a natural man is or can be the subject of while he remains in a natural state, and also from everything that men or devils can be the authors of. It is a spiritual work in this high sense, and therefore, above all other works, it belongs to the Spirit of God alone. There is no work so high and excellent. For there is no work in which God imparts himself so much, and in which the mere creature has, in so high a sense, a participation of God. So it is expressed in Scripture by the saints #emph[being made partakers of the divine nature] (2 Pet 1:4); #emph[having God dwelling in them, and they in God] (1 John 4:12, 15, 16, and ch. 3:24); #emph[having Christ in them] (John 17:21; Rom 8:10); #emph[being the temples of the living God] (2 Cor 6:16); #emph[living by Christ’s life] (Gal 2:20); #emph[being made partakers of God’s holiness] (Heb 12:10); #emph[having Christ’s love dwelling in them] (John 17:26); #emph[having his joy fulfilled in them] (John 17:13); #emph[seeing light in God’s light,] and #emph[being made to drink of the river of God’s delights] (Ps 36:8–9); #emph[having fellowship with God,] or #emph[sharing and partaking with him] (as the word signifies) (1 John 1:3). This does not mean that the saints are made partakers of the essence of God, and so are #emph[godded] with God and #emph[christed] with Christ, according to the abominable and blasphemous language and notions of some heretics. But, to use the Scripture’s phrase, they are made partakers of God’s #emph[fullness] (Eph 3:17–19; John 1:16), that is, of God’s spiritual beauty and happiness, according to the measure and capacity of a creature; for it is evident that this is what the word #emph[fullness] signifies in the language of Scripture. Grace in the hearts of the saints, then, is the most glorious work of God, in which he imparts of the goodness of his nature. So it is no doubt his own distinctive work, and in an eminent way above the power of all creatures. And the influences of the Spirit of God in this are peculiar to God in this way, and are those in which God, in so high a manner, imparts himself and makes the creature partaker of the divine nature (the Spirit of God imparting itself in its own proper nature). This is what I mean by influences that are #emph[divine,] when I say that #emph[truly gracious affections arise from influences that are spiritual and divine.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Only the true saints have what is spiritual; others have nothing that is divine, in the sense that has been spoken of. Not only do they lack these impartings of the Spirit of God in so high a #emph[degree] as the saints have them; they have nothing of that #emph[nature] or #emph[kind.] For the apostle #emph[James] tells us that #emph[natural] men #emph[do not have the Spirit.] And Christ teaches the necessity of a new birth, or being born of the Spirit, from this: that #emph[he who is born of the flesh has] only #emph[flesh,] and no #emph[Spirit] (John 3:6). They do not have the Spirit of God dwelling in them in any degree. For the apostle teaches that all who have the Spirit of God dwelling in them belong to Christ (Rom 8:9–11). And having the Spirit of God is spoken of as a certain sign that people will have the eternal inheritance, for it is spoken of as the earnest of it (2 Cor 1:22 and 5:5; Eph 1:14). And having anything #emph[of the Spirit] is mentioned as a sure sign of being in Christ (1 John 4:13): “By this we know that we dwell in Him, because He has given us #emph[of] His Spirit.” Ungodly men not only have less of the divine nature than the saints; they are not #emph[partakers of it] at all, which implies that they have nothing of it. For being a #emph[partaker] of the divine nature is spoken of as the special privilege of the true saints (2 Pet 1:4). Ungodly men are not #emph[partakers of God’s holiness] (Heb 12:10). A natural man has no experience of any of the things that are spiritual. The apostle teaches us that he is so far from it that he knows nothing about them. He is a complete stranger to them; talk about such things is all foolishness and nonsense to him; he does not know what it means (1 Cor 2:14): “The natural man does not accept the things that come from the Spirit of God. For they are foolishness to him, and he cannot know them, because they are spiritually discerned.” And to the same purpose Christ teaches us that the world is wholly unacquainted with the Spirit of God (John 14:17): “the Spirit of truth. The world cannot receive Him, because it neither sees Him nor knows Him.” And it is further evident that natural men have nothing in them of the same nature as the true grace of the saints, because the apostle teaches us that those of them who go furthest in religion have #emph[no charity,] or true Christian love (1 #emph[Cor] chapter 13). So Christ elsewhere rebukes the Pharisees, those high pretenders to religion, because they #emph[did not have the love of God in them] (John 5:42). So natural men have no communion or fellowship with Christ, no participation with him (as these words signify), for this is spoken of as the special privilege of the saints (1 John 1:3, together with verses 6–7; and 1 Cor 1:8–9). And the Scripture speaks of the actual existence of a gracious principle in the soul, even in its first beginning, as a seed planted there, as inconsistent with a man’s being a sinner (1 John 3:9). And natural men are represented in Scripture as having no spiritual light, no spiritual life, and no spiritual being. That is why conversion is often compared to opening the eyes of the blind, raising the dead, a work of creation (in which creatures are made entirely new), and becoming newborn children.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[From these things it is evident that the gracious influences the saints are the subjects of, and the effects of God’s Spirit that they experience, are entirely above nature. They are altogether of a different kind from anything that men find within themselves by nature, or only in the exercise of natural principles. They are things to which no improvement of natural qualities or principles, no advancing or raising of them to higher degrees, and no kind of combining of them, will ever bring men. For they differ from what is natural, and from everything that natural men experience, not only in degree and circumstances, but also in kind, and are of a vastly more excellent nature. And this is what I mean by #emph[supernatural,] when I say that #emph[gracious affections come from influences that are supernatural.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[From this it follows that in the gracious exercises and affections produced in the minds of the saints through the saving influences of the Spirit of God, there is a new inward #emph[perception] or #emph[sensation] of their minds, entirely different in its nature and kind from anything their minds were ever the subjects of before they were sanctified. For suppose God by his mighty power produces something that is new, not only in degree and circumstances, but in its whole nature, something that could not be produced by any raising, varying or combining of what was there before, or by adding anything of the same kind. If God produces something new in this way in a mind, which is a perceiving, thinking, conscious thing, then no doubt something entirely new is felt, or perceived, or thought. Or, which is the same thing, there is some new sensation or perception of the mind, entirely of a new sort, which could not be produced by any raising, varying or combining of the kind of perceptions or sensations the mind had before. Or there is what some metaphysicians call a new #emph[simple idea.] If grace is, in the sense described above, an entirely new kind of principle, then its exercises are also an entirely new kind of exercises. And if there are in the soul a new sort of exercises that it is conscious of, which the soul knew nothing of before, and which no improvement, combination or management of what it was conscious or aware of before could produce, nor anything like them, then it follows that the mind has an entirely new kind of perception or sensation. Here there is, as it were, a new #emph[spiritual sense] that the mind has, or a principle of a new kind of perception or spiritual sensation, which in its whole nature is different from any former kinds of sensation of the mind, as tasting is different from any of the other senses. And in the exercise of this new sense of mind, a true saint perceives something in spiritual and divine things as entirely different from anything natural men perceive in them as the sweet taste of honey is different from the ideas men get of honey by only looking at it and feeling it. So the spiritual perceptions that a sanctified and spiritual person has are different from all that natural men have, not only in the way the ideas or perceptions of the same sense may differ from one another, but rather as the ideas and sensations of different senses differ. That is why the work of the Spirit of God in regeneration is often compared in Scripture to giving a new sense: giving eyes to see and ears to hear, unstopping the ears of the deaf, opening the eyes of those who were born blind, and turning from darkness to light. And because this spiritual sense is immensely the most noble and excellent, and without it all other principles of perception and all our faculties are useless and vain, the giving of this new sense, with its blessed fruits and effects in the soul, is compared to raising the dead and to a new creation.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This new spiritual sense, and the new dispositions that attend it, are not new #emph[faculties,] but new #emph[principles] of nature. I use the word #emph[principles] for want of a word with a more definite meaning. By a #emph[principle of nature] in this place I mean the foundation laid in nature, either old or new, for any particular manner or kind of exercise of the faculties of the soul; or a natural habit or foundation for action, giving a person ability and disposition to exert the faculties in exercises of a certain kind, so that to exert the faculties in that kind of exercises may be said to be his nature. So this new spiritual sense is not a new faculty of understanding, but a new foundation laid in the nature of the soul for a new kind of exercises of the same faculty of understanding. In the same way, the new holy disposition of heart that attends this new sense is not a new faculty of will, but a foundation laid in the nature of the soul for a new kind of exercises of the same faculty of will.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The Spirit of God, in all his operations on the minds of natural men, only moves, impresses, assists, improves, or in some way acts on #emph[natural principles;] he gives no new #emph[spiritual principle.] So when the Spirit of God gives a natural man visions, as he did #emph[Balaam,] he only impresses a natural principle, namely the sense of sight, directly stirring up ideas of that sense. But he gives no new sense, and there is nothing supernatural, spiritual or divine in it. So too, if the Spirit of God impresses on a man’s imagination, either in a dream or when he is awake, any outward ideas of any of the senses, whether voices or shapes and colors, it is only stirring up ideas of the same kind that he has by natural principles and senses. So too, if God reveals to a natural man some secret fact, for instance something that he will later see or hear, this is not infusing or exercising any new spiritual principle, or giving the ideas of any new spiritual sense. It is only impressing, in an extraordinary way, the ideas that will later be received by sight and hearing. So also in the more ordinary influences of the Spirit of God on the hearts of sinners, he only assists natural principles to do to a greater degree the same work that they do of themselves by nature. So the Spirit of God by his common influences may assist men’s natural ingenuity, as he assisted #emph[Bezalel] and #emph[Oholiab] in the skilled works of the tabernacle. So he may assist men’s natural abilities in political affairs, and improve their courage and other natural qualities, as he is said to have put his Spirit on the seventy elders, and on #emph[Saul,] so as to #emph[give him another heart.] So God may greatly assist natural men’s reason in their reasoning about worldly things or about the doctrines of religion, and may greatly advance the clearness of their perceptions and notions of the things of religion in many respects, without giving any spiritual sense. So in the awakenings and convictions that natural men may have, God only assists conscience, which is a natural principle, to do to a further degree the work it does naturally. Conscience naturally gives men a grasp of right and wrong, and suggests the connection there is between right and wrong and a retribution. The Spirit of God assists men’s consciences to do this to a greater degree, and helps conscience against the numbing influence of worldly objects and of their lusts. And many other ways might be mentioned in which the Spirit acts on, assists and moves natural principles. But after all, it is no more than nature moved, acted on and improved; there is nothing supernatural and divine here. But the Spirit of God, in his spiritual influences on the hearts of his saints, works by infusing or exercising new, divine and supernatural principles: principles that are truly a new and spiritual nature, and vastly more noble and excellent than anything in natural men.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[From what has been said it follows that all spiritual and gracious affections are attended by, and arise from, some perception, idea or sensation of mind that in its whole nature is different, indeed exceedingly different, from anything that is or can be in the mind of a natural man. The natural man discerns nothing of it and has no idea of it at all (as 1 Cor 2:14 says). He can conceive of it no more than a man without the sense of taste can conceive of the sweet taste of honey, or a man without the sense of hearing can conceive of the melody of a tune, or a man born blind can have a notion of the beauty of the rainbow.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But here two things must be observed in order to understand this rightly.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. On the one hand it must be observed that not everything that in any respect belongs to spiritual affections is new and entirely different from what natural men can conceive of and do experience. Some things are common to gracious affections and other affections; many circumstances, accompaniments and effects are common. So a saint’s love to God has a great many things belonging to it that it shares with a man’s natural love for a near relative. Love to God makes a man desire the honor of God and desire to please him; so a natural man’s love for his friend makes him desire his friend’s honor and desire to please him. Love to God causes a man to delight in thoughts of God, to delight in the presence of God, and to desire conformity to God and the enjoyment of God; and so it is with a man’s love for his friend. And many other things might be mentioned that are common to both. Yet the idea the saint has of the loveliness of God, and the sensation and the kind of delight he has in that view, which is, as it were, the marrow and quintessence of his love, is peculiar to him, and entirely different from anything a natural man has or can have any notion of. And even in the things that seem to be common there is something distinctive. Both spiritual love and natural love cause desires for the object loved, but they are not the same sort of desires. There is a sensation of soul in the spiritual desires of one who loves God that is entirely different from all natural desires. Both spiritual love and natural love are attended by delight in the object loved, but the sensations of delight are not the same; they are entirely and exceedingly different. Natural men may have conceptions of many things #emph[about] spiritual affections. But there is something in them, which is, as it were, their #emph[nucleus] or kernel, of which natural men have no more conception than one born blind has of colors.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It may be clearly illustrated in this way. Suppose two men: one is born without the sense of taste, and the other has it. The second loves honey and is greatly delighted in it, because he knows its sweet taste; the first loves certain sounds and colors. The love of each has many things belonging to it that are common: it causes both of them to desire and delight in the object loved, causes grief when it is absent, #emph[and so on.] Yet the idea or sensation that the one who knows the taste of honey has of its excellence and sweetness, which is the foundation of his love, is entirely different from anything the other has or can have. And the delight he has in honey is wholly different from anything the other can conceive of, though they both delight in the objects they love. So too, both these persons may in some respects love the same object. The one may love a delicious kind of fruit, which is beautiful to the eye and delicious to the taste, not only because he has seen its pleasant colors, but because he knows its sweet taste. The other, completely ignorant of this, loves it only for its beautiful colors. There are many things that seem in some respect to be common to both: both love, both desire, and both delight. But the love and desire and delight of the one is altogether different from that of the other. The difference between the love of a natural man and that of a spiritual man is like this, except that it must be observed that in one respect it is vastly greater. That is, the kinds of excellence these different kinds of persons perceive in spiritual objects are in themselves vastly more different than the different kinds of excellence perceived in delicious fruit by a man who #emph[tastes] and a man who has #emph[no taste.] And in another respect it may not be so great: the spiritual man may have a spiritual sense or taste to perceive that divine and most distinctive excellence only in small beginnings and in a very imperfect degree.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. On the other hand, it must be observed that a natural man may have religious perceptions and affections that are in many respects very new and surprising to him, things he had not conceived of before; and yet what he experiences may be nothing like the exercises of a principle of new nature, or the sensations of a new spiritual sense. His affections may be very new, by natural principles being moved in an extraordinary way, to a very new degree, with a great many new circumstances, a new cooperation of natural affections, and a new combination of ideas. This may come from some extraordinarily powerful influence of #emph[Satan] and some great delusion; but there is nothing in it except nature acted on in an extraordinary way. Suppose a poor man who had always lived in a cottage, and had never looked beyond the obscure village where he was born, were taken as a joke to a magnificent city and a prince’s court. Suppose he were dressed there in princely robes and set on the throne, with the royal crown on his head and peers and nobles bowing before him, and were made to believe that he was now a glorious monarch. The ideas he would have and the affections he would experience would in many respects be very new, such as he had never imagined before. But all of it is no more than natural principles raised and stirred in an extraordinary way, and ideas of the sort he has by nature newly heightened, varied and combined. There is nothing here like giving him a new sense.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[On the whole, I think it is clearly evident that all truly gracious affections arise from special and distinctive influences of the Spirit, producing in the souls of the saints a #emph[felt effect] or #emph[sensation] that is entirely different from anything it is possible for a natural man to experience, different not only in degree and circumstances, but in its whole nature. So a natural man not only cannot experience what is individually the same; he cannot experience anything except what is exceedingly different from it, and immensely below it, in its kind. And it is something that the power of men or devils is not sufficient to produce, nor anything like it, nor anything of the same nature.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I have dwelt at length on this matter because it is of great importance and use for clearly exposing and demonstrating the delusions of #emph[Satan] in many kinds of false religious affections, by which multitudes are deluded, and probably have been in all ages of the Christian church; and for settling and determining many articles of doctrine concerning the operations of the Spirit of God and the nature of true grace.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now therefore, to apply these things to the purpose of this discourse.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[From this it appears that impressions some have made on their imagination, or the imaginary ideas they have of God, or Christ, or heaven, or anything belonging to religion, have nothing in them that is spiritual or of the nature of true grace. Though such things may attend what is spiritual and be mixed with it, yet in themselves they have nothing spiritual, nor are they any part of gracious experience.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Here, for the sake of the common people, I will explain what is meant by #emph[impressions on the imagination] and #emph[imaginary ideas.] The imagination is the power of the mind by which it can have a conception or idea of things of an external or outward nature (that is, of the sort of things that are the objects of the outward senses) when those things are not present and are not perceived by the senses. It is called imagination from the word #emph[image,] because by it a person can have an image of some external thing in his mind when that thing is not present in reality, nor anything like it. All the kinds of things we perceive by our five external senses, #emph[seeing, hearing, smelling, tasting] and #emph[feeling,] are external things. When a person has an idea or image of any of these sorts of things in his mind when they are not there, and when he does not really see, hear, smell, taste or feel them, that is to have an #emph[imagination] of them, and these ideas are #emph[imaginary ideas.] And when such ideas are strongly impressed on the mind, and their image in the mind is very lively, almost as if one saw them or heard them, #emph[and so on,] that is called an #emph[impression on the imagination.] So colors and shapes and the form of a face are outward things, because they are the sort of things that are the objects of the outward sense of sight. And therefore when anyone has in his mind a lively idea of any shape or color or form of a face, that is to have an imagination of those things. So too, if he has an idea of the sort of light or darkness that he perceives by the sense of sight, that is to have an idea of outward light, and so it is an imagination. So too, if he has an idea of any marks made on paper, say letters and words written in a book, that is to have an external and imaginary idea of the kind of things we sometimes perceive with our bodily eyes. And when we have ideas of the kind of things we perceive by any of the other senses, such as sounds or voices or spoken words, this is only to have ideas of outward things, namely of the kind of things perceived by the external sense of hearing, and so that also is imagination. And when these ideas are vividly impressed, almost as if they were really heard with the ears, this is to have an impression on the imagination. And so I might go on and give examples of the ideas of things belonging to the other three senses, #emph[smelling, tasting] and #emph[feeling.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Many who have had such things have very ignorantly supposed them to be of the nature of spiritual manifestations. They have had lively ideas of some external shape and the beautiful form of a face, and this they call spiritually seeing Christ. Some have had ideas of a great outward light impressed on them, and this they call a spiritual manifestation of God’s or Christ’s glory. Some have had ideas of Christ hanging on the cross, with his blood running from his wounds, and this they call a spiritual sight of Christ crucified and of the way of salvation by his blood. Some have seen him with his arms open, ready to embrace them, and this they call a manifestation of the sufficiency of Christ’s grace and love. Some have had lively ideas of heaven, and of Christ on his throne there, and shining ranks of saints and angels, and this they call seeing heaven opened to them. Some have had, from time to time, a lively idea of a person with a beautiful face smiling on them, and this they call a spiritual manifestation of the love of Christ to their souls, and tasting the love of Christ. And they consider it sufficient evidence that these things are spiritual manifestations, and that they see them spiritually, because, they say, they do not see these things with their bodily eyes but in their hearts, for they can see them when their eyes are shut. In the same way, the imaginations of some have been impressed with ideas of the sense of hearing. They have had ideas of words, as if they were spoken to them, sometimes the words of Scripture and sometimes other words. They have had ideas of Christ speaking comforting words to them. These things they have called having the inward call of Christ, hearing the voice of Christ spiritually in their hearts, having the witness of the Spirit, and the inward testimony of the love of Christ, #emph[and so on.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The common sort of people, who are less thoughtful and understanding, are the more easily led to think that these things are spiritual things, for this reason: spiritual things are invisible, and not things that can be pointed out with the finger, so we are forced to use figurative expressions in speaking of them, and to borrow names from external and perceptible objects to signify them. So we call a clear perception of spiritual things by the name of #emph[light,] and having such an apprehension of this or that thing by the name of #emph[seeing] it. And the conviction of the judgment and the persuasion of the will by the word of Christ in the gospel we signify by #emph[spiritually hearing the call of Christ.] And the Scripture itself abounds with figurative expressions of this kind. People hear these used often, and have pressed on them the necessity of having their eyes opened, having a manifestation of spiritual things, seeing Christ in his glory, having the inward call, and the like. So they ignorantly look and wait for some such external manifestations and imaginary views as have been spoken of. And when they have them, they are confident that now their eyes are opened, now Christ has revealed himself to them, and they are his children. So they are exceedingly moved and elated by their deliverance and happiness, and many kinds of affections are set in violent motion in them all at once.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But it is exceedingly plain that such ideas have nothing in them that is spiritual and divine, in the sense in which it has been demonstrated that all gracious experiences are spiritual and divine. These external ideas are in no way of such a sort that they are entirely, and in their whole nature, different from everything men have by nature, completely different from and vastly above any sensation a man can possibly have by any natural sense or principle, so that, in order to have any sensations of that sort, a man must be given a new spiritual and divine sense. Far from it: they are ideas of the same sort we have by the external senses, which are among the inferior powers of human nature. They are merely ideas of external objects, or ideas of that nature, of the same outward, sensory kind. They are the same sort of sensations of mind (differing not in degree, but only in circumstances) that we have by the natural principles we share with the beasts, namely the five external senses. It is a low, miserable notion of spiritual sense to suppose that it is only conceiving or imagining the sort of ideas we have by our animal senses, senses that the beasts have in as great perfection as we do. It is, as it were, turning Christ, or the divine nature in the soul, into a mere animal. Nothing is lacking in the soul, as it is by nature, to make it capable of being the subject of all these external ideas without any new principles. A natural man is capable of having an idea, and a lively idea, of shapes and colors and sounds when they are absent, and as capable as a regenerate man is; so there is nothing supernatural in them. And it is known by abundant experience that it is not the advancing or perfecting of human nature that makes people more capable of having such lively and strong imaginary ideas. On the contrary, weakness of body and mind, and diseases of the body, make people far more susceptible to such impressions #footnote[Fancies and whims abound most in men of weak reason; children, and those who are cracked in their understanding, have the most of them; strength of reason banishes them, as the sun does mists and vapors. But the more rational any gracious person is, the more he is fixed and settled and satisfied in the grounds of religion: indeed, there is the highest and purest reason in religion; and when this change is worked in men, it is carried on in a rational way, Isa 1:18; John 19:9. #emph[Flavel’s] preparation for sufferings, ch. 6.].]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As to a truly spiritual sensation, not only is the way it comes into the mind extraordinary, but the sensation itself is totally different from anything men have or can have in a state of nature, as has been shown. But as to these external ideas, though the way they come into the mind is sometimes unusual, the ideas in themselves are not the better for that. They are still of no different sort from what men have by their senses; they are of no higher kind, and not a bit better. For instance, the external idea a man has now of Christ hanging on the cross and shedding his blood is no better in itself than the external idea the #emph[Jews,] his enemies, had, who stood around his cross and saw this with their bodily eyes. The imaginary idea men have now of an external brightness and glory of God is no better than the idea the wicked congregation in the wilderness had of the external glory of the Lord at Mount #emph[Sinai,] when they saw it with bodily eyes. Nor is it any better than the idea that millions of cursed reprobates will have of the external glory of Christ at the day of judgment, who will see, and have a very lively idea of, an external glory of Christ ten thousand times greater than was ever yet conceived in any man’s imagination #footnote[If any man should see and behold Christ really, immediately, this is not the saving knowledge of him. I know the saints do know Christ as if immediately present; they are not strangers because of their distance: if others have seen him more immediately, I will not dispute it. But if they have seen the Lord Jesus as immediately as if he were here on earth, yet #emph[Capernaum] saw him so; indeed, some of them were disciples for a time, and followed him, #emph[John] 6. And yet the Lord was hidden from their eyes. Indeed, all the world will see him in his glory, which will amaze them; and yet this falls far short of having the saving knowledge of him, which the Lord imparts to the elect. So though you see the Lord so really that you become familiar with him, yet, Luke 13:26, “We ate and drank with you,” and so perish. #emph[Shepard’s] Par. Of the ten virgins, P. I. P. 197, 198.]. Indeed, the image of Christ that men conceive in their imaginations is not, in its own nature, of any higher kind than the idea the Papists conceive of Christ from the beautiful and moving images of him they see in their churches (though the way they receive the idea may not be so bad). Nor are the affections men have, if they are built primarily on such imaginations, any better than the affections raised in ignorant people by the sight of those images, which are often very great, especially when these images, through the craft of the priests, are made to move and speak and weep, and the like #footnote[#emph[Satan] is transformed into an angel of light: and so we have heard that some have heard voices; some have seen the very blood of Christ dropping on them, and the wounds in his side; some have seen a great light shining in the room; some have been wonderfully moved by their dreams; some in great distress have had an inward witness, “your sins are forgiven”; and from this such liberty and joy that they are ready to leap up and down the room. O adulterous generation! This is natural and usual with men: they would gladly see Jesus, and have him present to give them peace; and that is why Papists have his images.—Woe to those who have no other manifested Christ but such a one. #emph[Shepard’s] Par. Of the ten virgins, P. I. P. 198.]. The mere way in which people receive these imaginary ideas does not alter the nature of the ideas themselves that are received. Let them be received in whatever way they may, they are still only external ideas, or ideas of outward appearances, and so they are not spiritual. Indeed, if men should actually receive such external ideas by the immediate power of the Most High God on their minds, they would not be spiritual; they would be no more than a common work of the Spirit of God. This is evident in fact in the case of #emph[Balaam.] God himself impressed on his mind a clear and lively outward representation or idea of Jesus Christ, as #emph[the star rising out of] Jacob, when #emph[he heard the words of God, and knew the knowledge of the Most High, and saw the vision of the Almighty, falling into a trance] (Num 24:16–17). Yet he had no spiritual manifestation of Christ of any kind. That morning star never rose spiritually in his heart, for he was only a natural man.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as these external ideas have nothing divine or spiritual in their nature, and nothing but what natural men are capable of without any new principles, so there is nothing in their nature that requires, for their production, that distinctive, inimitable and unparalleled exercise of the glorious power of God which, as has been shown, there is in the production of true grace. There appears to be nothing in their nature above the power of the devil. It is certainly not above the power of #emph[Satan] to suggest thoughts to men, for otherwise he could not tempt them to sin. And if he can suggest any thoughts or ideas at all, no doubt imaginary ones, or ideas of external things, are not above his power #footnote[Consider how difficult, indeed impossible, it is to determine that such a voice, vision or revelation is from God, and that #emph[Satan] cannot imitate or counterfeit it; seeing he has left no certain marks by which we may distinguish one spirit from another. #emph[Flavel’s] causes and cures of mental errors, cause 14.]\; for the external ideas men have are the lowest sort of ideas. These ideas may be raised merely by impressions made on the body, by moving the animal spirits and impressing the brain. Abundant experience certainly shows that changes in the body will stir up imaginary or external ideas in the mind, as often happens in a high fever, melancholy, #emph[and so on.] These external ideas are as far below the more intellectual exercises of the soul as the body is a less noble part of man than the soul.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And not only is there nothing in the nature of these external ideas, or imaginations of outward appearances, from which we can infer that they are above the power of the devil; it is also certain that the devil can stir up such ideas, and has often done so. They were external ideas that he stirred up in the dreams and visions of the false prophets of old, who were under the influence of lying spirits, whom we often read of in Scripture, as in Deut 13:1; 1 Kgs 22:22; Isa 28:7; Ezek 13:7; Zech 13:4. And they were external ideas that he often stirred up in the minds of the heathen priests, magicians and sorcerers in their visions and ecstasies. And they were external ideas that he stirred up in the mind of the man Christ Jesus when he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory, when those kingdoms were not really in sight.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And if #emph[Satan,] or any created being, has power to impress the mind with outward representations, then no particular sort of outward representation can be any evidence of a divine power. Almighty power is no more required to represent the shape of a man to the imagination than the shape of anything else. No higher kind of power is needed to form one bodily shape or color in the brain than another. It takes no more glorious power to represent the form of a man’s body than the form of a chip or a block, even if it is a very beautiful human body, with a sweet smile on its face, or arms open, or blood running from hands, feet and side. The sort of power that can represent black or darkness to the imagination can also represent white and shining brightness. The power and skill that can paint a straw or a stick of wood well and exactly on a piece of paper or canvas will be enough, the same in kind, only perhaps further developed, to paint the body of a man with great beauty and in royal majesty, or a magnificent city paved with gold, full of brightness, and a glorious throne, #emph[and so on.] So it takes no more than the same sort of power to paint the one of these on the brain as the other. The same sort of power that can put ink on paper can put on gold leaf. So it is evident to a demonstration, if we suppose it is in the devil’s power to make any sort of external representation at all on the imagination (as no doubt it is, and no one who believed there was a devil with any agency among mankind ever questioned it): I say, if this is so, it is demonstrably evident that a created power may extend to all kinds of external appearances and ideas in the mind.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[From this it again clearly appears that no such things have anything in them that is spiritual, supernatural and divine, in the sense in which it has been proved that all truly gracious experiences are. And though external ideas, because of man’s make and frame, ordinarily attend spiritual experiences in some degree, yet these ideas are no part of the spiritual experience, any more than the motion of the blood and the beating of the pulse that attend experiences are a part of spiritual experience. And no doubt, through men’s infirmity in the present state, and especially through the weak constitution of some people, gracious affections that are very strong do stir up lively ideas in the imagination. Yet it is also beyond doubt that when people’s affections are #emph[founded on] imaginations, which is often the case, those affections are merely natural and common, because they are built on a foundation that is not spiritual. And so they are entirely different from gracious affections, which, as has been proved, always arise from operations that are spiritual and divine.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[These imaginations often raise men’s carnal affections to an exceedingly great height #footnote[There is a remarkable passage of Mr. #emph[John Smith,] in his discourse on the shortness of a Pharisaic righteousness, p. 370, 371, of his select discourses, describing the sort of religion that is built on such a foundation as I am speaking of here. I cannot keep from copying the whole of it. Speaking of a sort of Christians whose life is nothing but a strong energy of fancy, he says: so that their religion may not too grossly show itself to be nothing but a piece of art, there may at times be such extraordinary motions stirred up within them, going before all their own thoughts, that they may seem to be a true operation of the divine life; when yet all this is nothing but the energy of their own self-love, touched with some fleshly perceptions of divine things, and stirred up by them. There are things in our Christian religion which, when a carnal, unhallowed mind takes the chair and gets to expound them, may seem very delicious to the fleshly appetites of men: some doctrines and notions of free grace and justification, the magnificent titles of sons of God and heirs of heaven, ever-flowing streams of joy and pleasure that blessed souls will swim in to all eternity, a glorious paradise in the world to come, always springing up with sweet-scented and fragrant beauties, a new #emph[Jerusalem] paved with gold and spangled with stars, taking in within its vast circuit such numberless varieties that a busy curiosity may occupy itself with them to all eternity. I do not doubt that at times the most fleshly and earthly men, who in their ambition fly to the pomp of this world, may be so ravished with fancies of such things as these that they may seem to be made partakers of the powers of the world to come. I do not doubt that they might be much exalted by them, as the souls of crazed or deranged people seem to be at times, when their fancies play with those quick and nimble spirits that a diseased state of body and an unnatural heat in their heads produce within them. So these blazing comets may rise up above the moon and climb higher than the sun; yet because they have no solid substance of their own, and are of a base and earthly alloy, they will soon vanish and fall down again, being held up only by an external force. They may seem to themselves to have reached higher than those noble Christians who are gently moved by the natural force of true goodness: they seem to be #emph[pleniores Deo] \[#emph[that is,] more full of God\] than those who are really formed and moved by the divine Spirit, and move on steadily and constantly in the way toward heaven. It is as with the seed that was sown on stony ground, which grew up and lengthened out its blade faster than the seed sown in the good and fruitful soil. And while our souls are in this mortal condition, sunk deeply into the body, the motions of our sense and fancy and passions are many times more vigorous, and make stronger impressions on us, than those of the higher powers of the soul, which are more subtle and further removed from these mixed animal perceptions. In the same way, the devotion that is seated there may seem to have more energy and life in it than the devotion that spreads itself gently, with a more delicate kind of touch, over the understanding, and from there flows mildly through our wills and affections. But however much more boisterous the former may be for a time, the latter is of a more lasting, seed-like and thriving nature. For the former, coming in fact from nothing but a sensory and fleshly perception of God and of true happiness, is only of a fleeting and fading nature; and as the sensory powers and faculties grow more languid, or the sun of divine light shines more brightly on us, these earthly devotions, like our kitchen fires, will lose their heat and fervor. But a true heavenly warmth will never be put out, because it is of an immortal nature; and once it is seated vitally in the souls of men, it will regulate and order all the soul’s motions in a proper way, as the natural heat rooted in the hearts of living creatures has the rule and management of the whole body under it. True religion is no piece of artifice; it is no boiling up of our imaginative powers, nor the glowing heats of passion, though these are too often mistaken for it, when in our trickery in religion we cast a mist before our own eyes. But it is a new nature, forming the souls of men; it is a godlike frame of spirit, showing itself most of all in serene and clear minds, in deep humility, meekness, self-denial, universal love to God and all true goodness, without partiality and without hypocrisy, by which we are taught to know God, and knowing him to love him, and to conform ourselves as far as possible to all the perfection that shines in him.]. And no wonder, when those who have them are ignorantly but without doubt persuaded that they are divine manifestations, which the great Jehovah makes directly to their souls, giving them in an extraordinary way testimonies of his high and special favor.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Again, from what has been observed and proved about the way in which gracious operations and effects in the heart are spiritual, supernatural and divine, it is evident that the immediate suggesting of words of Scripture to the mind has nothing in it that is spiritual.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I have had occasion to say something about this already, and what has been said may be enough to prove it. But if the reader keeps in mind what has been said about the nature of spiritual influences and effects, it will be still more abundantly evident that this is no spiritual effect. For I suppose there is no person of common understanding who will say or imagine that bringing words to the mind (whatever words they may be) is an effect of a kind that the mind of a natural man, while he remains in a state of nature, cannot possibly be the subject of, or anything like it; or that it requires any new divine sense in the soul; or that bringing sounds or letters to the mind is an effect of so high, holy and excellent a nature that no created power can possibly be its cause.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Suggesting words of Scripture to the mind is only stirring up in the mind ideas of certain sounds or letters, and so it is only one way of stirring up ideas in the imagination; for sounds and letters are external things, the objects of the external senses of sight and hearing. Ideas of certain marks on paper, such as any of the twenty-four letters in whatever order, or any sounds of the voice, are just as much external ideas as ideas of any other shapes or sounds whatever. And therefore, by what has already been said about these external ideas, it is evident that they are nothing spiritual. And if at any time the Spirit of God suggests these letters or sounds to the mind, this is a #emph[common] influence of that Spirit, and not any #emph[special] or #emph[gracious] one. And therefore it follows from what has already been proved that the affections that have this effect for their foundation are not spiritual or gracious affections. But let it be noted what it is I am saying, namely: when this effect, the immediate and extraordinary #emph[manner of words of Scripture coming to the mind,] is what stirs up the affections, and is properly their foundation, then these affections are not spiritual. It may be that people have gracious affections that go along with Scriptures that come to their minds, and the Spirit of God may make use of those Scriptures to stir them up. That is the case when what stirs up their affections is some spiritual sense, taste or relish they have of the divine and excellent things contained in those Scriptures, and not the extraordinary and sudden way the words are brought to their minds. They are moved by the instruction they receive from the words, and by the view of the glorious things of God or Christ, and the things belonging to them, that the words contain and teach. They are not moved because the words came suddenly, as though some person had spoken them to them, and they concluded from this that God was, as it were, speaking to them directly. People are often exceedingly moved on this foundation. The words of some great and high promises of Scripture come suddenly to their minds, and they regard the words as directed by God immediately to them, as though the words came out of the mouth of God at that moment, spoken to them. So they take it as a voice from God, immediately revealing to them their happy situation and promising this or that great thing to them; and this is what moves and elates them. There is no new spiritual understanding of the divine things #emph[contained in the Scripture,] nor any new spiritual sense of the glorious things taught in that part of the Bible, going before their affection and being its foundation. All the new understanding they have, or think they have, as the foundation of their affection is this: that the words #emph[are spoken to them,] because they come so suddenly and extraordinarily. And so this affection is built wholly on the sand, because it is built on a conclusion for which they have no foundation. For, as has been shown, the sudden coming of the words to their minds is no evidence that their being brought to their minds in that way was from God. And even if it were true that God brought the words to their minds, and they knew it with certainty, that would not be spiritual knowledge; it may exist without any spiritual sense. #emph[Balaam] might know that the words God suggested to him were indeed suggested to him by God, and yet have no spiritual knowledge. So the affections that are built on the notion that texts of Scripture are sent immediately from God are built on no spiritual foundation, and are vain and delusive. If people whose affections are raised in this way were asked whether they have any new sense of the excellence of the things contained in those Scriptures, they would probably say yes, without hesitation. But it is true in no other way than this: once they have taken up the notion that the words are spoken directly to them, that makes the words seem sweet to them, and they acknowledge the things these Scriptures say to them to be excellent and wonderful things. For instance, suppose these were the words suddenly brought to their minds: “Do not be afraid, … it is your#super[+] Father’s good pleasure to give you#super[+] the kingdom.” They have confidently taken up the notion that the words were, as it were, spoken directly from heaven to them, as an immediate revelation that God was their Father and had given the kingdom to them. So they are greatly moved by it, and the words seem sweet to them. And oh, they say, #emph[what excellent things are contained in those words!] But the only reason the promise seems excellent to them is that they think it is made directly to them. All the sense they have of any glory in the words comes only from #emph[self-love,] and from their own imagined share in them. It is not that they had any view or sense of the holy and glorious nature of the kingdom of heaven, and of the spiritual glory of the God who gives it, and of his excellent grace to sinful men in offering and giving them this kingdom of his own good pleasure, going before their imagined share in these things and their being moved by them, and being the foundation of their affection and of their hope of a share in them. On the contrary, they first imagine they have a share in it, and then are highly moved by that, and then can acknowledge these things to be excellent. So the sudden and extraordinary way the Scriptures come to their minds is plainly the first foundation of the whole. This is clear evidence of the wretched delusion they are under.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The first comfort of many people, and what they call their conversion, comes in this way. After awakening and terrors, some comforting, sweet promise comes suddenly and wonderfully to their minds, and the way it comes makes them conclude that it comes from God #emph[to them.] And this is the very thing that is the whole foundation of their faith and hope and comfort. From this they take their first encouragement to trust in God and in Christ, because they think that God, by some Scripture brought in this way, has now already revealed to them that he loves them, and has already promised them eternal life. This is very absurd. For everyone with a common knowledge of the principles of religion knows that it is God’s way to reveal his love to men, and their share in the promises, after they have believed, and not before; because they must first believe before they have any share in the promises to be revealed. The Spirit of God is a Spirit of truth, and not of lies. He does not bring Scriptures to men’s minds to reveal to them that they have a share in God’s favor and promises when they have none, because they have not yet believed. Yet this would be the case if God’s bringing texts of Scripture to men’s minds to reveal to them that their sins were forgiven, or that it was God’s pleasure to give them the kingdom, or anything of that kind, went before their first faith and was its foundation. No promise of the covenant of grace belongs to any man until he has first believed in Christ. For it is by faith alone that we come to have a share in Christ and in the promises of the new covenant made in him. And therefore whatever spirit applies the promises of that covenant to a person who has not first believed, as being already his, must be a lying spirit; and the faith that is first built on such an application of promises is built on a lie. It is not God’s way to bring comforting texts of Scripture to give men #emph[assurance] of his love, and that they will be happy, before they have had a faith of #emph[dependence] #footnote[Mr. #emph[Stoddard,] in his Guide to Christ, p. 8, says that at times men, after they have been in trouble for a while, have some promises come to them with a great deal of refreshment, and they hope God has accepted them; and he says that in this case the minister may tell them that God never gives a faith of assurance before he gives a faith of dependence; for he never manifests his love until men are in a state of favor and reconciliation, which is by faith of dependence. When men have comforting Scriptures come to them, they are apt to take them as tokens of God’s love; but men must be brought into Christ, by accepting the offer of the gospel, before they are fit for such manifestations. God’s method is first to make the soul accept the offers of grace, and then to manifest its good state to it. And p. 76, speaking of those who seem to be brought to lie at God’s feet, and who give an account of their embracing Christ, and say that God has revealed Christ to them and drawn their hearts to him, and that they do accept Christ, he says that in this case it is best to examine whether, by the light that was given him, he saw Christ and salvation offered to him, or whether he saw that God loved him or pardoned him: for the offer of grace and our acceptance go before pardon, and therefore much more before the knowledge of it. Mr. #emph[Shepard,] in his parable of the ten virgins, Part II p. 15, says that grace and the love of Christ (the fairest colors under the sun) may be pretended; but if you receive, under this appearance, that God witnesses his love first by an absolute promise, take care there; for under this appearance you may just as well bring in immediate revelations, and from there come to forsake the Scriptures. And in Part I, p. 86, he says: Is Christ yours? Yes, I see it. How? By any word or promise? No: this is delusion. And p. 136, speaking of those who have no solid ground of peace, he counts among them those who content themselves with the revelation of the Lord’s love without the sight of any work, or without looking to it. And he says soon after: the testimony of the Spirit does not make a man more a Christian, but only shows it to be so; as it is the nature of a witness, not to make a thing true, but to clear it and show it. And p. 140, speaking of those who say they have the witness of the Spirit, which makes a difference between them and hypocrites, he says: the witness of the Spirit does not make the first difference; for first a man is a believer, and in Christ, and justified, called and sanctified, before the Spirit witnesses it; otherwise the Spirit would witness to an untruth, and lie.]. And if the Scripture that comes to a person’s mind is not properly a promise so much as an invitation, yet if he makes the sudden or unusual way the invitation came to his mind the ground on which he believes he is invited, it is not true faith, because it is built on what is not the true ground of faith. True faith is built on no uncertain foundation. But a judgment that the words of a particular text were suggested to the mind by the immediate power of God at a certain time, as though they were then spoken and directed by God to him, because the words came in a certain way, is a wholly uncertain and unreliable judgment, as has now been shown. And therefore it is a false and sandy foundation for faith, and accordingly the faith built on it is false. The only certain foundation any person has for believing that he is invited to partake of the blessings of the gospel is that the word of God declares that people qualified as he is are invited, and the God who declares it is true and cannot lie. If a sinner is once convinced of the truthfulness of God, and that the Scriptures are his word, he will need nothing more to convince and satisfy him that he is invited. For the Scriptures are full of invitations to sinners, to the chief of sinners, to come and partake of the benefits of the gospel. He will not need any new speaking of God to him; what God has already spoken will be enough for him.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As the first comfort of many people, and their affections at the time of their supposed conversion, are built on grounds like those just mentioned, so are their joys and hopes and other affections from time to time afterward. They often have particular words of Scripture, sweet declarations and promises, suggested to them, which, because of the way they come, they think are sent #emph[immediately] from God to them #emph[at that time.] They regard this as their warrant to take them, and they actually make it the main ground of applying them to themselves, of the comfort they take in them, and of the confidence they receive from them. So they imagine that a kind of conversation is carried on between God and them, and that God from time to time, as it were, speaks to them directly, satisfies their doubts, testifies his love to them, promises them support and supplies and his blessing in this or that case, and reveals to them clearly their share in eternal blessings. And so they are often elated, and have a run of sudden and tumultuous kinds of joy, mingled with a strong confidence and a high opinion of themselves. Yet in fact the main ground of these joys and this confidence is not anything #emph[contained in] or #emph[taught by] these Scriptures as they stand in the Bible, but the #emph[way they came to them.] This is certain evidence of their delusion. There is no particular promise in the word of God that is the saint’s, or is made or spoken to him, in any other way than all the promises of the covenant of grace are his, and are made and spoken to him #footnote[Mr. #emph[Shepard,] in his sound believer, p. 159, of the late printing at #emph[Boston,] says: embrace in your bosom not only a few promises, but all. And then he asks the question: when may a Christian take a promise as spoken to him without presumption? He answers: the rule is very sweet, but certain; when he takes all the Scripture and embraces it as spoken to him, he may then take any particular promise boldly. My meaning is, when a Christian takes hold of, and wrestles with God for, the fulfillment of all the promises of the New Testament; when he sets all the commands before him as a compass and guide to walk by; when he applies all the threatenings to drive him nearer to Christ, the end of them. This no hypocrite can do; this the saints will do; and by this they may know when the Lord speaks in particular to them.]. It is true that some of these promises may be more especially suited to his case than others, and God by his Spirit may enable him to understand some better than others, and to have a greater sense of the preciousness and glory and suitableness of the blessings contained in them.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But here some may be ready to say, “What! Is there no such thing as a particular spiritual application of the promises of Scripture by the Spirit of God?” I answer, there is no doubt such a thing as a spiritual and saving application of the invitations and promises of Scripture to the souls of men. But it is also certain that its nature is wholly misunderstood by many people, to the great ensnaring of their own souls, and giving #emph[Satan] a vast advantage against them, and against the interest of religion and the church of God. The spiritual application of a promise of Scripture does not consist in its being immediately suggested to the thoughts by some outside agent, and borne into the mind with a strong persuasion that it is spoken and directed particularly to them at that time. There is nothing of the evidence of the hand of God in this effect, as events have proved in many notorious instances. It is a poor notion of a spiritual application of Scripture. There is nothing at all in its nature beyond the power of the devil, if he is not restrained by God; for there is nothing in the nature of the effect that is spiritual, implying any vital imparting of God. A truly spiritual application of the word of God is of a vastly higher nature, as far above the devil’s power as it is to apply the word of God to a dead corpse so as to raise it to life, or to a stone so as to turn it into an angel. A spiritual application of the word of God consists in #emph[applying it to the heart,] in spiritually enlightening, sanctifying influences. A spiritual application of an invitation or offer of the gospel consists in giving the soul a spiritual sense or relish of the holy and divine blessings offered. It gives a sense also of the sweet and wonderful grace of the one who offers them, in making so gracious an offer, and of his holy excellence and faithfulness to fulfill what he offers, and his glorious sufficiency for it. So it leads and draws out the heart to embrace the offer, and in this way gives the man evidence of his title to the thing offered. And in the same way, a spiritual application of the promises of Scripture, for the comfort of the saints, consists in enlightening their minds to see the holy excellence and sweetness of the blessings promised, and also the holy excellence of the one who promises, and his faithfulness and sufficiency. So it draws out their hearts to embrace the one who promises and the thing promised; and by this means it gives the felt actings of grace, enabling them to see their grace, and so their title to the promise. An application that does not consist in this divine sense and enlightening of the mind, but consists only in the words being borne into the thoughts as if they were spoken directly at that moment, and so makes people believe, on no other foundation, that the promise is theirs, is a blind application. It belongs to the spirit of darkness, and not of light.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[When people have their affections raised in this way, those affections are really not raised by the word of God; the Scripture is not their foundation. It is not anything contained in the Scriptures that come to their minds that raises their affections. It is in fact that effect, namely the strange way the words were suggested to their minds, and a proposition they take up from it which is not actually contained in that Scripture or in any other: such as that #emph[his] sins are forgiven him, or that it is the Father’s good pleasure to give #emph[him in particular] the kingdom, or the like. There are propositions to be found in the Bible declaring that people with such and such qualifications are forgiven and loved by God. But there are no propositions to be found in the Bible declaring that such and such particular persons are forgiven and loved by God, apart from any previous knowledge of any qualifications. And therefore when any person is comforted and moved by any such proposition, it is by another word, a newly coined word, and not by any word of God contained in the Bible. #footnote[Some Christians have rested in a work without Christ, which is abominable: but after a man is in Christ, not to judge by the work is, first, not to judge from a word. For though there is a word that may give a man a #emph[dependence] on Christ without feeling any work, indeed when he feels none, as absolute promises; yet there is no word giving #emph[assurance] except one that is made to some work: “#emph[He who believes,]” or, “Blessed are the #emph[poor in spirit,]” and so on; until that work is seen, he has no assurance from that promise. #emph[Shepard’s] parable of the ten virgins. Part I. P. 86. If God should tell a saint that he has grace, he might know it by believing the word of God: but it is not in this way that godly men know that they have grace; it is not revealed in the word, and the Spirit of God does not testify it to particular persons. #emph[Stoddard’s] nature of saving conversion, p. 84, 85.] And so many people are moved in vain and deluded.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Again, it plainly appears from what has been demonstrated #emph[that no revelation of secret facts by immediate suggestion] is anything spiritual and divine, in the sense in which gracious effects and operations are.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[By #emph[secret facts] I mean things that have been done, or have come to pass, or will come to pass hereafter, which are secret in the sense that they do not appear to the senses, and are not known by any reasoning or any evidence to reason, nor in any other way, but only by revelation, by the immediate suggestion of the ideas of them to the mind. For instance, suppose it were revealed to me that next year this land would be invaded by a fleet from #emph[France,] or that such and such persons would then be converted, or that I myself would then be converted. Suppose this were done not by enabling me to reason out these events from anything that now appears in providence, but by immediately suggesting and bearing in on my mind, in an extraordinary way, the perception or ideas of these facts, with a strong suggestion or impression on my mind, which I had no hand in myself, that these things would come to pass. Or suppose it were revealed to me that a battle is being fought today between the armies of such and such powers in #emph[Europe;] or that such a prince in #emph[Europe] was converted today, or is now in a converted state, having been converted before; or that one of my neighbors is converted, or that I myself am converted. Suppose I had no other evidence of any of these facts from which to reason them out, but only an immediate, extraordinary suggestion or stirring up of these ideas, and a strong impression of them on my mind. This is a revelation of secret facts by immediate suggestion just as much as if the facts were future. For whether the facts are past, present or future does not alter the case, as long as they are secret and hidden from my senses and reason, not spoken of in Scripture, and not known by me in any other way than by #emph[immediate suggestion.] If it is revealed to me that such a revolution has come to pass today in the #emph[Ottoman] empire, it is the very same sort of revelation as if it were revealed to me that such a revolution would come to pass there a year from today. For though one is present and the other future, both are equally hidden from me, except by immediate revelation. When #emph[Samuel] told #emph[Saul] that the donkeys he went to look for had been found, and that his father had stopped worrying about the donkeys and was worrying about him, this was by the same kind of revelation as that by which he told #emph[Saul] that in the plain of #emph[Tabor] three men going up to God at #emph[Bethel] would meet him (1 Sam 10:2–3), though one of these things was future and the other was not. So when #emph[Elisha] told the king of #emph[Israel] the words that the king of #emph[Syria] spoke in his bedroom, it was by the same kind of revelation as that by which he foretold many things to come.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It is evident that this revelation of secret facts by immediate suggestion has nothing of the nature of a spiritual and divine operation, in the sense mentioned above. There is nothing at all in the nature of the perceptions or ideas themselves that are stirred up in the mind that is divinely excellent, and so is far above all the ideas of natural men, though the way the ideas are stirred up is extraordinary. In things that are spiritual, as has been shown, not only #emph[the way the effect is produced,] but #emph[the effect produced] is divine, and so vastly above anything that can be in an unsanctified mind. Now simply having an idea of facts, setting aside the way those ideas are produced, is nothing beyond what the minds of wicked men are capable of without any goodness in them. And all of them either have, or will have, the knowledge of the truth of the greatest and most important facts that have been, are, or will be.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as to the extraordinary way of producing the ideas or perception of facts, by immediate suggestion, there is nothing in it but what the minds of natural men, while they are still natural men, are capable of, as is evident in #emph[Balaam] and others spoken of in the Scripture. And therefore it appears that there is nothing belonging to this immediate suggestion of secret facts that is spiritual, in the sense in which it has been proved that gracious operations are spiritual. If there is nothing in the ideas themselves that is holy and divine, and so nothing but what may be in a mind not sanctified, then God can put them into the mind #emph[by immediate power] without sanctifying it. There is nothing in the idea of a rainbow itself that is of a holy and divine nature, so nothing prevents an unsanctified mind from receiving that idea. So God, if he pleases and when he pleases, may stir up that idea in an unsanctified mind #emph[immediately, and in an extraordinary way.] So too, there is nothing in the idea or knowledge that such and such particular persons are forgiven and accepted by God and entitled to heaven but what unsanctified minds may have, and will have, concerning many people at the day of judgment. So God can, if he pleases, suggest this to an unsanctified mind now, and impress it on it, extraordinarily and immediately. No principle is lacking in an unsanctified mind to make it capable of such a suggestion or impression; nor is there anything in such minds to exclude such a suggestion or necessarily to hinder it.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And if these suggestions of secret facts are accompanied by #emph[texts of Scripture,] immediately and extraordinarily brought to mind, about some other facts that seem similar in some respects, that does not make the operation spiritual and divine in nature. For that suggestion of words of Scripture is no more divine than the suggestion of the facts themselves, as has just been demonstrated. And two effects together, neither of which is spiritual, cannot make up one combined effect that is spiritual.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So it follows, from what has already been shown and often repeated, that affections properly founded on such immediate suggestions, or supposed suggestions, of secret facts are not gracious affections. It is possible that such suggestions may #emph[be the occasion,] or #emph[accidental cause,] of gracious affections, for a mistake and a delusion may be that. But such a suggestion is never properly the #emph[foundation] of gracious affections. For gracious affections, as has been shown, are all the effects of an influence and operation that is spiritual, supernatural and divine. But there are many affections, and high affections, that some people have which have suggestions or revelations of this kind for their very foundation. They regard these as spiritual manifestations, which is a gross delusion; and this delusion is truly the spring from which their affections flow.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Here it may be proper to observe that it is exceedingly evident from what has been said that what many people call the #emph[witness of the Spirit] that they are the children of God has nothing spiritual and divine in it, and consequently that the affections built on it are vain and delusive. What many call the witness of the Spirit is nothing other than an immediate suggestion and impression of the fact, otherwise secret, that they are converted, or made the children of God, and so that their sins are pardoned and that God has given them a title to heaven. This kind of knowledge, namely, knowing that a certain person is converted, delivered from hell, and entitled to heaven, is not a divine sort of knowledge in itself. This sort of fact does not require any higher or more divine kind of suggestion to impress it on the mind than any other fact that #emph[Balaam] had impressed on his mind. It requires no higher sort of idea or sensation for a man to have the perception of his own conversion impressed on him than to have the apprehension of his neighbor’s conversion impressed on him in the same way. But God, if he pleased, might impress the knowledge of this fact, that he had forgiven his neighbor’s sins and given him a title to heaven, as well as any other fact, without any imparting of his holiness. The excellence and importance of the fact does not at all prevent a natural man’s mind from being capable of an immediate suggestion and impression of it. #emph[Balaam] had facts as excellent and important and glorious as this impressed immediately on his mind, without any gracious influence: in particular, the coming of Christ, his setting up his glorious kingdom, and the blessedness of the spiritual #emph[Israel] in his special favor, and their happiness in living and dying. Indeed, #emph[Abimelech,] king of the Philistines, had God’s special favor to a particular person, #emph[Abraham,] revealed to him (Gen 20:6–7). So it seems that God revealed to #emph[Laban] his special favor to #emph[Jacob]\; see Gen 31:24 and Ps 105:15. And if a truly good man should have an immediate revelation or suggestion from God in the same way concerning God’s favor to his neighbor or to himself, it would be no higher kind of influence. It would be no more than a common sort of influence of God’s Spirit, as the gift of prophecy, and all revelation by immediate suggestion, is; see 1 Cor 13:2. It is true that a natural man cannot possibly have that individual suggestion from the Spirit of God, that he is converted, because it is not true. Yet that does not arise from the nature of the influence, or because the kind of influence that suggests such excellent facts is too high for him to be the subject of it, but purely from the lack of a fact to be revealed. The influence that immediately suggests this fact, when it is true, is of no different kind from the influence that immediately suggests other true facts. And so the kind and nature of the influence is not above what natural men share with good men.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But it is a poor, ignoble notion of the witness of the Spirit of God, given to his dear children, to suppose that there is nothing in the kind and nature of that influence of the Spirit of God in imparting this high and glorious benefit except what is common to natural men, or what men may be capable of while they are all the time altogether unsanctified and children of hell; and that therefore the benefit or gift itself has nothing of the holy nature of the Spirit of God in it, nothing of a vital imparting of that Spirit. This notion greatly debases the high and most exalted kind of influence and operation of the Spirit that there is in the true witness of the Spirit #footnote[The late venerable #emph[Stoddard,] in his younger years, falling in with the opinion of some others, accepted this notion of the witness of the Spirit by way of immediate suggestion; but in the latter part of his life, when he had weighed things more thoroughly and had more experience, he entirely rejected it, as appears from his treatise of the nature of saving conversion, p. 84: The Spirit of God does not testify to particular persons that they are godly. Some think that the Spirit of God does testify it to some; and they ground it on Rom 8:16: “The Spirit Himself bears witness with our spirit that we are God’s children.” They think the Spirit reveals it by giving an inward testimony to it; and some godly men think they have had experience of it: but they may easily be mistaken; when the Spirit of God eminently stirs up a spirit of faith, and pours out the love of God in the heart, it is easy to mistake it for a testimony. And that is not the meaning of #emph[Paul’s] words. The Spirit reveals things to us by opening our eyes to see what is revealed in the word; but the Spirit does not reveal new truths, not revealed in the word. The Spirit shows the grace of God in Christ, and by this draws out special actings of faith and love, which are evidential; but it does not work by way of testimony. If God only helps us to receive the revelations in the word, we will have comfort enough without new revelations.]. What is called #emph[the witness of the Spirit] in Rom 8 is elsewhere in the New Testament called #emph[the seal of the Spirit] (2 Cor 1:22; Eph 1:13 and 4:30). This alludes to the seal of princes, attached to the document by which they raised any of their subjects to some high honor and dignity, or special privilege in the kingdom, as a token of their special favor. This is evidence that the influence of the Spirit of the Prince of princes, in sealing his favorites, is far from being of a common kind. There is no effect of God’s Spirit whatever that is more divine in its nature, nothing more holy, more peculiar to God, more inimitable, and more distinguishing of divinity. For nothing is more royal than the royal seal; nothing belonging to a prince is more sacred, or more especially marks what belongs to him. It is the very end and purpose of the seal to be the most distinctive stamp and confirmation of the royal authority, the great mark of distinction by which what comes from the king, or belongs to him, may be known from everything else. And therefore no doubt the seal of the great King of heaven and earth, stamped on the heart, is something high and holy in its own nature, some excellent outflow from the infinite fountain of divine beauty and glory. It is not merely making known a secret fact by revelation or suggestion, which is a sort of influence of the Spirit of God that the children of the devil have often been the subjects of. The seal of the Spirit is a kind of effect of the Spirit of God on the heart that natural men, while they remain natural men, are so far from being capable of receiving that they can have no notion or idea of it at all. This agrees with Revelation 2:17: “To the one who overcomes, I will give the hidden manna. I will also give him a white stone inscribed with a new name, known only to the one who receives it.” There is every reason to suppose that what is spoken of here is the same mark, evidence or blessed token of special favor that is elsewhere called the #emph[seal of the Spirit.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[What has misled many in their notion of the influence of the Spirit of God we are speaking of is the word witness, its being called the #emph[witness] of the Spirit. So they have taken it to be, not any effect or work of the Spirit on the heart giving evidence from which men may reason that they are the children of God, but an inward, immediate suggestion, as though God spoke inwardly to the man, testified to him, and told him that he was his child, by a kind of secret voice or impression. They have not observed the way the word #emph[witness,] or #emph[testimony,] is often used in the New Testament, where such terms often signify not merely declaring and asserting a thing to be true, but holding out evidence from which a thing may be reasoned out and proved to be true. So in Heb 2:4 God is said to bear witness “through signs, wonders, various miracles, and gifts of the Holy Spirit.” Now the miracles spoken of here are called God’s witness, not because they are of the nature of assertions, but because they are evidences and proofs. So in Acts 14:3: “So Paul and Barnabas spent considerable time there, speaking boldly for the Lord, who bore witness to the message of His grace by enabling them to perform signs and wonders.” And in John 5:36: “But I have testimony more substantial than that of #emph[John.] For the works that the Father has given Me to accomplish—the very works I am doing—testify about Me that the Father has sent Me.” Again in ch. 10:25: “The works I do in My Father’s name testify on My behalf.” So the water and the blood are said to bear witness (1 John 5:8), not that they spoke or asserted anything, but they were proofs and evidences. So God’s works of providence, in the rain and fruitful seasons, are spoken of as witnesses of God’s being and goodness; #emph[that is,] they were evidences of these things. And when the Scripture speaks of the #emph[seal] of the Spirit, the expression properly denotes, not an immediate voice or suggestion, but some work or effect of the Spirit that is left as a divine mark on the soul, to be an evidence by which God’s children may be known. The seals of princes were the distinguishing marks of princes, and so God’s seal is spoken of as God’s mark, as in Rev 7:3: “Do not harm the land or sea or trees until we have sealed the foreheads of the servants of our God,” together with Ezekiel 9:4: “Put a mark on the foreheads of the men sighing and groaning over all the abominations committed there.” When God sets his seal on a man’s heart by his Spirit, there is some holy stamp, some image impressed and left on the heart by the Spirit, as by the seal on the wax. And this holy stamp, or impressed image, displaying clear evidence to the conscience that the one who bears it is a child of God, is the very thing that is called in Scripture #emph[the seal of the Spirit,] and #emph[the witness,] or #emph[evidence, of the Spirit.] And the image the Spirit stamps on the hearts of God’s children is his own image. That is the evidence by which they are known to be God’s children: that they have the image of their Father stamped on their hearts by the Spirit of adoption. In ancient times seals had two things engraved on them, namely the image and the name of the person whose seal it was. So when Christ says to his bride in Song 8:6, “Set me as a seal over your heart, as a seal upon your arm,” it is as much as to say, “Let my name and image remain impressed there.” The seals of princes used to bear their image, so that whatever they set their seal and royal mark on had their image left on it. It was the custom of princes of old to have their image engraved on their jewels and precious stones; and the image of #emph[Augustus] engraved on a precious stone was used as the seal of the Roman emperors in the times of Christ and the apostles. #footnote[See #emph[Chambers’s] dictionary, under the word times.] And the saints are the jewels of Jesus Christ, the great sovereign who possesses the empire of the universe; and these jewels have his image stamped on them by his royal signet, which is the Holy Spirit. And no doubt this is what the Scripture means by the seal of the Spirit, especially when it is stamped in so fair and clear a way as to be plain to the eye of conscience, which is what the Scripture calls #emph[our spirit.] This is truly an effect that is #emph[spiritual, supernatural] and #emph[divine.] In itself it is of a holy nature, being an imparting of the divine nature and beauty. The kind of influence of the Spirit that gives and leaves this stamp on the heart is such that no natural man can be the subject of anything of a like nature. This is the highest sort of witness of the Spirit that the soul can possibly be the subject of. If there were any such thing as a witness of the Spirit by immediate suggestion or revelation, this would be vastly more noble and excellent, as far above it as the heaven is above the earth. This the devil cannot imitate. But as to an inward suggestion of the Spirit of God, by a kind of secret voice speaking and immediately asserting and revealing a fact, he can produce something a thousand times more like that than anything he can produce like the holy and divine effect, or work, of the Spirit of God that has now been spoken of. #footnote[Mr. #emph[Shepard] argues abundantly against the notion of men knowing their good state by an immediate witness of the Spirit, without judging by any effect or work of the Spirit produced in the heart as an evidence and proof that people are the children of God. Part I. P. 134: #emph[“We know that He has chosen you.”] How so? Immediately? Some divines think that the angels do not see it so, and that it is peculiar to God to do so; but mediately: #emph[“because our gospel came to you … in power … and with much assurance,”] to make you enlarged for God, to make you turn “to God from idols,” and so on. Again on the same page: It is heaven to cleave to him in every command; it is death to depart from any command. #emph[“By this we know that we are in Him.”] If it were possible to ask the angels how they know they are not devils, they would answer, The Lord’s will is ours. So here: how do you know you do not have the nature of devils, and so are not in the state of devils, bound there until the judgment of the great day? Because God has changed our vile nature, and made our wills like his glorious will. And p. 135: The #emph[major] is the word, the #emph[minor] experience, and the #emph[conclusion] the work of the Lord’s Spirit, awakening your spirits to it. Now, some say, how do you know this? You may be mistaken in this way; for many have been deceived in this way. Grant that; and shall a child not take bread when it is given him, though dogs snatch at it? And p. 137: If you look to a Spirit without a work, while you seek consolation, you cannot avoid the condemnation of the word. You say the Spirit has spoken peace to you; but do you love Christ? I do not look to that, but to the Spirit. Why, the word says, #emph[“If anyone does not love the Lord, let him be under a curse.”] So, is the league between your sins and your souls broken? Answer: I do not look to that. Why, #emph[John] says, #emph[“The one who practices sin is of the devil.”] Are you new creatures? I do not look to that. Why, the word says that #emph[unless you are born again] you cannot enter the kingdom of God. Page 176, 177: A man says, I have Christ, and they do not. I ask, where is the Spirit? You have the deed; where is the seal? You have the testator; where is the executor? The Spirit in you? Yes, I have it; it has witnessed that Christ is mine. Answer: It has witnessed; but what has it worked? Where is the power of his death, killing your lusts? Where is the life of the Spirit of Jesus in you? Where is the oil in your vessel? Truly I look for the bridegroom; but I do not regard that; nor are others to regard it, by way of evidence. Then I say the chief evidence is destroyed in the churches. I have known many who have had assurances, yet I never saw them prove right until it witnessed that this was here. What can be the causes of this, and that men should make an uproar in the churches because of this, as though it were building on works? In different men they are different. 1. A readiness to outrun the truth, and to fall from one extreme to another.—2. The apostasy of eminent professing Christians, who have been deceived in their evidencing in this way.—3. Corrupt experience.—4. A heart that never felt the bitterness and bondage of sin as the greatest evil. Page 215, 216: The peace and joy and assurance of that glory which eye never saw, in the saints, is from the witness of the Spirit of glory; not only because God is their God, but because they are his people. It is, I say, from the witness of God in his word; not from themselves, nor from man only, that they approve me; nor from dreams and diabolical breathings; but from the Spirit of God; he brings news of it; and from such a Spirit (so that you may know it) as not only shows you that God is your God, and so you rejoice because of this (for so it is with many a carnal heart, and he has peace, after being in horror, from this, #emph[the Lord loves me]); but he makes you rejoice because you are the Lord’s people, because he has changed your heart: now the peace is found, and the joy is right: and here I would test the peace of any man. Part II. P. 168, 169: All the heirs of the promises, as heirs who have legacies left them, go to the will of the deceased Father; and what comforts them, what they hold to, is what is sure: such a one will have it, if his name is there. But if one should say, Such a one has promised me such lands—Is it in his will?—No; but since he died, as I was smoking a pipe, he came to me: oh, do not be deceived! Again, in his #emph[sound believer] there is a long discourse on sanctification as the chief evidence of justification, from p. 221 for many pages following; I will copy only a very small part of it. Tell me how you will know that you are justified. You will say, by the testimony of the Spirit. And cannot the same Spirit shine on your graces, and witness that you are sanctified as well? 1 John 4:13; 3:24; 1 Cor 2:12. Can the Spirit make the one clear to you and not the other? Oh, beloved, it is a sad thing to hear such questions, and such cold answers too, that sanctification possibly may be an evidence. May be! Is it not certain? Assuredly, to deny it is as bad as to affirm that God’s own promises of favor are not true evidences of it, and consequently that they are lies and untruths. Mr. #emph[Flavel] also strongly opposes this notion of the witness of the Spirit by immediate revelation. Sacramental meditations, Med. 4. Speaking of the sealing of the Spirit, he says: in sealing the believer, he does not make use of an audible voice, nor the ministry of angels, nor immediate and extraordinary revelations; but he makes use of his own graces, implanted in our hearts, and his own promises, written in the Scripture: and by this method he usually brings the doubting, trembling heart of a believer to rest and comfort. Again, #emph[Ibid.:] Assurance is produced in our souls by the reflective acts of faith: the Spirit helps us to reflect on what he has done in our hearts before; #emph[“By this we know that we know Him”] (1 John 2:3). To know that we know is a reflective act. Now it is impossible for there to be a reflective act before there has been a direct act. No man can have the evidence of his faith before the habit is infused and the vital act performed. The subject matter to which the Spirit sets his seal is his own sanctifying operation. Afterward, #emph[Ibid.,] he says: the immediate ways of the Spirit’s sealing have ceased. No man may now expect, by any new revelation or sign from heaven, by any voice or extraordinary inspiration, to have his salvation sealed; but he must expect that mercy in God’s ordinary way and method, searching the Scriptures, examining our own hearts, and waiting on the Lord in prayer. The learned #emph[Gerson] gives the example of one who had long been on the borders of despair, and at last was sweetly assured and settled: he answered, #emph[Non ex nova aliqua revelation;] not by any new revelation, but by subjecting my understanding to the written word, and comparing my heart with it. And Mr. #emph[Roberts,] in his treatise of #emph[the covenants,] speaks of another who panted so intensely after the sealings and assurance of the love of God to his soul that for a long time he earnestly desired some voice from heaven; and at times, walking in the solitary fields, he earnestly desired some miraculous voice from the trees or stones there. This was denied him; but in time a better thing was given, in a scriptural way. Again, #emph[Ibid.:] This method of sealing is beyond all other methods in the world. For in miraculous voices and inspirations it is possible that there may #emph[subess falsum,] that there may be found some cheat or imposture of the devil; but the Spirit’s witness in the heart, agreeing with the revelation in the Scripture, cannot deceive us.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Another thing that fully proves that the seal of the Spirit is no revelation of any fact by immediate suggestion, but is grace itself in the soul, is that in Scripture the #emph[seal of the Spirit] is called the #emph[earnest of the Spirit.] It is very plain from 2 Corinthians 1:22 that the seal of the Spirit is the same thing as the earnest of the Spirit: God “placed His seal on us, and put His Spirit in our hearts as an earnest of what is to come.” And from Eph 1:13–14: “In Him, having believed, you#super[+] were sealed with the promised Holy Spirit, who is the earnest of our inheritance until the redemption of the purchased possession, to the praise of His glory.” Now the earnest is part of the money agreed on, paid in hand as a token of the whole, which is to be paid in due time: a part of the promised inheritance, granted now as a token of full possession of the whole hereafter. But surely the kind of imparting of the Spirit of God that is of the nature of eternal glory is the highest and most excellent kind of imparting. It is something in its own nature spiritual, holy and divine, and far from anything common; and therefore it is high above anything of the nature of inspiration, or revelation of hidden facts by suggestion of the Spirit of God, which many natural men have had. What is the earnest and beginning of glory but grace itself, especially in its more lively and clear exercises? It is not prophecy, nor tongues, nor knowledge, but that more excellent divine thing, #emph[charity, the love that never fails,] which is a foretaste and beginning of the light, sweetness and blessedness of heaven, that world of love or charity. It is grace that is the seed of glory and the dawning of glory in the heart, and therefore it is grace that is the earnest of the future inheritance. What is the beginning or earnest of eternal life in the soul but spiritual life? And what is that but grace? The inheritance Christ has purchased for the elect is the Spirit of God, not in any extraordinary gifts, but in his vital indwelling in the heart, exerting and imparting himself there in his own proper, holy or divine nature. And this is the sum total of the inheritance Christ purchased for the elect. For things are so ordered in the matter of our redemption that the Father provides the Savior, or purchaser, and the purchase is made from him; the Son is the purchaser and the price; and the Holy Spirit is the great blessing or inheritance purchased, as is indicated in Gal 3:13–14. That is why the Spirit is often spoken of as the sum of the blessings promised in the gospel (Luke 24:49; Acts 1:4 and 2:38–39; Gal 3:14; Eph 1:13). This inheritance was the great legacy that Christ left his disciples and church in his last will and testament (#emph[John] chapters 14, 15 and 16). This is the sum of the blessings of eternal life that will be given in heaven. (Compare John 7:37–39 and John 4:14 with Rev 21:6 and 22:1, 17.) It is through the vital imparting and indwelling of the Spirit that the saints have all their light, life, holiness, beauty and joy in heaven. And it is through the vital imparting and indwelling of the same Spirit that the saints have all their light, life, holiness, beauty and comfort on earth, only imparted in less measure. And this vital indwelling of the Spirit in the saints, in this less measure and small beginning, is #emph[the earnest of the Spirit, the earnest of the future inheritance, and the firstfruits of the Spirit,] as the apostle calls it in Rom 8:23. There, by #emph[the firstfruits of the Spirit,] the apostle no doubt means the same vital, gracious principle he speaks of in all the earlier part of the chapter, which he calls Spirit and sets against flesh, or corruption. Therefore this earnest of the Spirit and firstfruits of the Spirit, which has been shown to be the same as the seal of the Spirit, is the vital, gracious, sanctifying imparting and influence of the Spirit, and not any immediate suggestion or revelation of facts by the Spirit. #footnote[After a man is in Christ, not to judge by the work is not to judge by the Spirit. For the apostle makes the earnest of the Spirit to be the seal. Now earnest is part of the money bargained for: the beginning of heaven, of its light and life. He who does not see that the Lord is his by that sees no God as his at all. Oh, therefore, do not look for a Spirit without a word to reveal, nor for a word to reveal without first seeing and feeling some work. I thank the Lord, I only pity those who think otherwise. If you are a sheep of Christ, oh, do not wander. #emph[Shepard’s] parable, P. I. P. 86.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And indeed, when the apostle in Rom 8:16 speaks of the Spirit bearing witness with our spirit that we are the children of God, he explains himself sufficiently, if only his words were attended to. What is expressed here is connected with the two verses before, as following from what the apostle had said there, as every reader may see. The three verses together read like this: “For all who are led by the Spirit of God are sons of God. For you#super[+] did not receive a spirit of bondage that returns you#super[+] to fear, but you#super[+] received the Spirit of adoption, by whom we cry, ‘Abba! Father!’ The Spirit Himself bears witness with our spirit that we are God’s children.” Here what the apostle says, if we take it together, plainly shows that what he has in view, when he speaks of the Spirit giving us witness or evidence that we are God’s children, is his dwelling in us and leading us as a spirit of adoption, or spirit of a child, disposing us to behave toward God as toward a Father. This is the witness or evidence the apostle speaks of, that we #emph[are children:] that we #emph[have the spirit of children,] or spirit of adoption. And what is that but the spirit of love? The apostle speaks of two kinds of spirit: the spirit of a slave, or #emph[the spirit of bondage,] which is #emph[fear;] and the spirit of a child, or #emph[Spirit of adoption,] which is #emph[love.] The apostle says we have not received the spirit of bondage, or of slaves, which is a spirit of fear; but we have received the more noble and generous spirit of children, a spirit of love, which naturally disposes us to go to God as children go to a father, and to behave toward God as children. And this is the evidence or witness the Spirit of God gives us that we are children. This is the plain sense of the apostle. And so no doubt the apostle here is speaking of the very same way of casting out doubting and #emph[fear] and the spirit of bondage that the apostle #emph[John] speaks of in 1 John 4:18, namely by the prevailing of #emph[love,] which is the spirit of a child. The spirit of bondage works by fear; the slave fears the rod. But love cries #emph[“Abba! Father!”] It disposes us to go to God and behave toward God as children, and it gives us clear evidence of our union with God as his children, and so it casts out fear. So it appears that the witness of the Spirit the apostle speaks of is far from being any whisper, or immediate suggestion or revelation. It is that gracious, holy effect of the Spirit of God in the hearts of the saints, the disposition and character of children, appearing in sweet childlike love to God, which casts out fear, or the spirit of a slave.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And the same thing is evident from the whole context. It is plain that the apostle speaks of the Spirit over and over again as dwelling in the hearts of the saints as a gracious principle, set against the flesh, or corruption. And so he does in the words that immediately introduce the passage we are considering, verse 13: “For if you#super[+] live according to the flesh, you#super[+] will die; but if by the Spirit you#super[+] put to death the deeds of the body, you#super[+] will live.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Indeed, I have no doubt that the apostle has a more special regard to the spirit of grace, or the spirit of love, or spirit of a child, in its more lively actings. For it is only #emph[perfect love,] or #emph[strong love,] that witnesses or gives evidence that we are children in such a way as to cast out fear and deliver us wholly from the spirit of bondage. The strong and lively exercises of a spirit of childlike, evangelical, humble love to God give clear evidence of the soul’s relation to God as his child, and this satisfies the soul very greatly and directly. It is far from true that in this case the soul judges only by an immediate witness, without any sign or evidence; for it judges, and is assured, by the greatest sign and the clearest evidence. Yet in this case the saint has no need of many signs, or of any long reasoning on them. And though his sight of his #emph[relative union] with God, and of his being in God’s favor, is not without a means, because he sees it by means of his love, yet his sight of the #emph[union of his heart] to God is immediate. Love, the bond of union, is seen intuitively. The saint sees and feels plainly the union between his soul and God; it is so strong and lively that he cannot doubt it. And from this he is assured that he is a child. How can he doubt whether he stands in a childlike relation to God, when he plainly sees a childlike union between God and his soul, and so boldly, and as it were naturally and necessarily, cries, #emph[“Abba! Father!”]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And where the apostle says the Spirit bears witness #emph[with our spirit,] by #emph[our spirit] here is meant our conscience, which is called the spirit of man in Prov 20:27: “The spirit of a man is the lamp of the #smallcaps[Lord], searching out his inmost being.” We read elsewhere of the witness of this spirit of ours, in 2 Cor 1:12: “This is our rejoicing: the testimony of our conscience.” And in 1 John 3:19–21: “And by this we will know that we belong to the truth, and will assure our hearts in His presence. For if our hearts condemn us, God is greater than our hearts, and He knows all things. Beloved, if our hearts do not condemn us, we have confidence before God.” When the apostle #emph[Paul] speaks of the Spirit of God bearing witness with our spirit, he is not to be understood as speaking of two spirits that are two separate, parallel, independent witnesses. Rather, it is by the one that we receive the witness of the other. The Spirit of God gives the evidence, by infusing and pouring out the love of God, the spirit of a child, in the heart; and our spirit, or our conscience, receives this evidence and declares it, for our rejoicing.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Many evils have arisen from the false and delusive notion of the witness of the Spirit, that it is a kind of inward voice, suggestion or declaration from God to a man that he is loved by him, and pardoned, elected, or the like, sometimes with a text of Scripture and sometimes without. And many false and vain (though very high) affections have arisen from it. And it is to be feared that multitudes of souls have been eternally ruined by it. That is why I have dwelt longer on this head.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But I go on now to a second characteristic of gracious affections.]]
